  "question": {
    "text": "In a triangle $\\mathrm{PQR}, \\angle \\mathrm{R}=\\pi / 2$. If $\\tan \\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{p}}{2}\\right)$ and $\\tan \\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{Q}}{2}\\right)$ are roots of $\\mathrm{ax}^2+\\mathrm{bx}+\\mathrm{c}=0$, where $\\mathrm{a} \\neq 0$, then which one is true ?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "d5SkY",
      "text": "$c=a+b$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "YlcCz",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{a}=\\mathrm{b}+\\mathrm{c}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "UX51K",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{b}=\\mathrm{a}+\\mathrm{c}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "LEE9e",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{b}=\\mathrm{c}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Hints: $\\frac{\\mathrm{P}}{2}+\\frac{\\mathrm{Q}}{2}=\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\frac{\\mathrm{P}}{2}=\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\tan \\left(\\frac{\\rho}{2}+\\frac{Q}{2}\\right)=1, \\frac{-b / a}{1-c / a}=1 \\Rightarrow \\frac{-b}{a-c}=1 \\\\\n& -b=a-c \\Rightarrow a+b=c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "image": null
